      <h2 id="preparing-an-initial-cluster-deployment-for-mce-disconnected_preparing-an-agent-based-installed-cluster-for-mce" class="group scroll-mt-20">Preparing an Agent-based cluster deployment for the multicluster engine for Kubernetes Operator while disconnected<a href="#preparing-an-initial-cluster-deployment-for-mce-disconnected_preparing-an-agent-based-installed-cluster-for-mce" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can mirror the required OpenShift Container Platform container images, the multicluster engine Operator, and the Local Storage Operator (LSO) into your local mirror registry in a disconnected environment. Ensure that you note the local DNS hostname and port of your mirror registry.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>To mirror your OpenShift Container Platform image repository to your mirror registry, you can use either the <code>oc adm release image</code> or <code>oc mirror</code> command. In this procedure, the <code>oc mirror</code> command is used as an example.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Create an <code>&lt;assets_directory&gt;</code> folder to contain valid <code>install-config.yaml</code> and <code>agent-config.yaml</code> files. This directory is used to store all the assets.</li>
      <li>
      To mirror an OpenShift Container Platform image repository, the multicluster engine, and the LSO, create a <code>ImageSetConfiguration.yaml</code> file with the following settings:
      <CodeBlock code={"  kind: ImageSetConfiguration\n  apiVersion: mirror.openshift.io/v1alpha2\n  archiveSize: 4\n  storageConfig:\n    imageURL: <your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/mirror/oc-mirror-metadata\n    skipTLS: true\n  mirror:\n    platform:\n      architectures:\n        - \"amd64\"\n      channels:\n        - name: stable-4.22\n          type: ocp\n    additionalImages:\n      - name: registry.redhat.io/ubi9/ubi:latest\n    operators:\n      - catalog: registry.redhat.io/redhat/redhat-operator-index:v4.22\n        packages:\n          - name: multicluster-engine\n          - name: local-storage-operator"} lang="yaml" title="Example ImageSetConfiguration.yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>archiveSize</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the maximum size, in GiB, of each file within the image set.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>storageConfig</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the back-end location to receive the image set metadata. This location can be a registry or local directory. It is required to specify <code>storageConfig</code> values.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>storageConfig.imageURL</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the registry URL for the storage backend.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>channels.name</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the channel that contains the OpenShift Container Platform images for the version you are installing.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>operators.catalog</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the Operator catalog that contains the OpenShift Container Platform images that you are installing.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>packages</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies only certain Operator packages and channels to include in the image set. Remove this field to retrieve all packages in the catalog. In this example, a <code>package.name</code> value of <code>multicluster-engine</code> includes the multicluster engine packages and channels, and <code>local-storage-operator</code> includes the LSO packages and channels.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>This file is required by the <code>oc mirror</code> command when mirroring content.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      To mirror a specific OpenShift Container Platform image repository, the multicluster engine, and the LSO, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc mirror --dest-skip-tls --config ocp-mce-imageset.yaml docker://<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Update the registry and certificate in the <code>install-config.yaml</code> file:
      <CodeBlock code={"  imageContentSources:\n    - source: \"quay.io/openshift-release-dev/ocp-release\"\n      mirrors:\n        - \"<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/openshift/release-images\"\n    - source: \"quay.io/openshift-release-dev/ocp-v4.0-art-dev\"\n      mirrors:\n        - \"<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/openshift/release\"\n    - source: \"registry.redhat.io/ubi9\"\n      mirrors:\n        - \"<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/ubi9\"\n    - source: \"registry.redhat.io/multicluster-engine\"\n      mirrors:\n        - \"<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/multicluster-engine\"\n    - source: \"registry.redhat.io/rhel8\"\n      mirrors:\n        - \"<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/rhel8\"\n    - source: \"registry.redhat.io/redhat\"\n      mirrors:\n        - \"<your-local-registry-dns-name>:<your-local-registry-port>/redhat\""} lang="yaml" title="Example imageContentSources.yaml" />
      <p>Additionally, ensure your certificate is present in the <code>additionalTrustBundle</code> field of the <code>install-config.yaml</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"additionalTrustBundle: |\n  -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\n  zzzzzzzzzzz\n  -----END CERTIFICATE-------"} lang="yaml" title="Example install-config.yaml" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The <code>oc mirror</code> command  creates a folder called <code>oc-mirror-workspace</code> with several outputs. This includes the <code>imageContentSourcePolicy.yaml</code> file that identifies all the mirrors you need for OpenShift Container Platform and your selected Operators.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Generate the cluster manifests by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openshift-install agent create cluster-manifests"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>This command updates the cluster manifests folder to include a <code>mirror</code> folder that contains your mirror configuration.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="preparing-an-initial-cluster-deployment-for-mce-connected_preparing-an-agent-based-installed-cluster-for-mce" class="group scroll-mt-20">Preparing an Agent-based cluster deployment for the multicluster engine for Kubernetes Operator while connected<a href="#preparing-an-initial-cluster-deployment-for-mce-connected_preparing-an-agent-based-installed-cluster-for-mce" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Create the required manifests for the multicluster engine Operator, the Local Storage Operator (LSO), and to deploy an agent-based OpenShift Container Platform cluster as a hub cluster.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a sub-folder named  <code>openshift</code> in the <code>&lt;assets_directory&gt;</code> folder. This sub-folder is used to store the extra manifests that will be applied during the installation to further customize the deployed cluster. The <code>&lt;assets_directory&gt;</code> folder contains all the assets including the <code>install-config.yaml</code> and <code>agent-config.yaml</code> files.
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The installer does not validate extra manifests.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      For the multicluster engine, create the following manifests and save them in the <code>&lt;assets_directory&gt;/openshift</code> folder:
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: v1\n  kind: Namespace\n  metadata:\n    labels:\n      openshift.io/cluster-monitoring: \"true\"\n    name: multicluster-engine"} lang="yaml" title="Example mce_namespace.yaml" />
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1\n  kind: OperatorGroup\n  metadata:\n    name: multicluster-engine-operatorgroup\n    namespace: multicluster-engine\n  spec:\n    targetNamespaces:\n    - multicluster-engine"} lang="yaml" title="Example mce_operatorgroup.yaml" />
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\n  kind: Subscription\n  metadata:\n    name: multicluster-engine\n    namespace: multicluster-engine\n  spec:\n    channel: \"stable-2.3\"\n    name: multicluster-engine\n    source: redhat-operators\n    sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace"} lang="yaml" title="Example mce_subscription.yaml" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You can install a distributed unit (DU) at scale with the Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) using the assisted installer (AI). These distributed units must be enabled in the hub cluster. The AI service requires persistent volumes (PVs), which are manually created.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      For the AI service, create the following manifests and save them in the <code>&lt;assets_directory&gt;/openshift</code> folder:
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: v1\n  kind: Namespace\n  metadata:\n    annotations:\n      openshift.io/cluster-monitoring: \"true\"\n    name: openshift-local-storage"} lang="yaml" title="Example lso_namespace.yaml" />
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1\n  kind: OperatorGroup\n  metadata:\n    name: local-operator-group\n    namespace: openshift-local-storage\n  spec:\n    targetNamespaces:\n      - openshift-local-storage"} lang="yaml" title="Example lso_operatorgroup.yaml" />
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\n  kind: Subscription\n  metadata:\n    name: local-storage-operator\n    namespace: openshift-local-storage\n  spec:\n    installPlanApproval: Automatic\n    name: local-storage-operator\n    source: redhat-operators\n    sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace"} lang="yaml" title="Example lso_subscription.yaml" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>After creating all the manifests, your filesystem must display as follows:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"<assets_directory>\n    ├─ install-config.yaml\n    ├─ agent-config.yaml\n    └─ /openshift\n        ├─ mce_namespace.yaml\n        ├─ mce_operatorgroup.yaml\n        ├─ mce_subscription.yaml\n        ├─ lso_namespace.yaml\n        ├─ lso_operatorgroup.yaml\n        └─ lso_subscription.yaml"} lang="terminal" title="Example Filesystem" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the agent ISO image by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openshift-install agent create image --dir <assets_directory>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>When the image is ready, boot the target machine and wait for the installation to complete.</li>
      <li>
      To monitor the installation, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openshift-install agent wait-for install-complete --dir <assets_directory>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>To configure a fully functional hub cluster, you must create the following manifests and manually apply them by running the command <code>$ oc apply -f &lt;manifest-name&gt;</code>. The order of the manifest creation is important and where required, the waiting condition is displayed.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      For the PVs that are required by the AI service, create the following manifests:
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: local.storage.openshift.io/v1\n  kind: LocalVolume\n  metadata:\n   name: assisted-service\n   namespace: openshift-local-storage\n  spec:\n   logLevel: Normal\n   managementState: Managed\n   storageClassDevices:\n     - devicePaths:\n         - /dev/vda\n         - /dev/vdb\n       storageClassName: assisted-service\n       volumeMode: Filesystem"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Use the following command to wait for the availability of the PVs, before applying the subsequent manifests:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc wait localvolume -n openshift-local-storage assisted-service --for condition=Available --timeout 10m"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <CodeBlock code={"The `devicePath` is an example and may vary depending on the actual hardware configuration used."} lang="plaintext" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a manifest for a multicluster engine instance.
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: multicluster.openshift.io/v1\n  kind: MultiClusterEngine\n  metadata:\n    name: multiclusterengine\n  spec: {}"} lang="yaml" title="Example MultiClusterEngine.yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a manifest to enable the AI service.
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: agent-install.openshift.io/v1beta1\n  kind: AgentServiceConfig\n  metadata:\n    name: agent\n    namespace: assisted-installer\n  spec:\n   databaseStorage:\n    storageClassName: assisted-service\n    accessModes:\n    - ReadWriteOnce\n    resources:\n     requests:\n      storage: 10Gi\n   filesystemStorage:\n    storageClassName: assisted-service\n    accessModes:\n    - ReadWriteOnce\n    resources:\n     requests:\n      storage: 10Gi"} lang="yaml" title="Example agentserviceconfig.yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a manifest to deploy subsequently spoke clusters.
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: hive.openshift.io/v1\n  kind: ClusterImageSet\n  metadata:\n    name: \"4.22\"\n  spec:\n    releaseImage: quay.io/openshift-release-dev/ocp-release:4.22.0-x86_64"} lang="yaml" title="Example clusterimageset.yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a manifest to import the agent installed cluster (that hosts the multicluster engine and the Assisted Service) as the hub cluster.
      <CodeBlock code={"  apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n  kind: ManagedCluster\n  metadata:\n   labels:\n     local-cluster: \"true\"\n     cloud: auto-detect\n     vendor: auto-detect\n   name: local-cluster\n  spec:\n   hubAcceptsClient: true"} lang="yaml" title="Example autoimport.yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Wait for the managed cluster to be created.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc wait -n multicluster-engine managedclusters local-cluster --for condition=ManagedClusterJoined=True --timeout 10m"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To confirm that the managed cluster installation is successful, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get managedcluster\nNAME            HUB ACCEPTED   MANAGED CLUSTER URLS             JOINED   AVAILABLE  AGE\nlocal-cluster   true           https://<your cluster url>:6443   True     True       77m"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
